{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-chunking-extra-whitespace", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "How   does chunking   split a document?", "max_steps": 4, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "done", "expected_stop_reason": "evidence_sufficient", "expected_min_citations": 1, "expected_max_citations": 1, "expected_source_paths": ["docs/ingest.md"], "expected_chunk_ids": ["chunking-1"], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "chunking-whitespace", "rationale": "Runs of spaces are separators to the word regex, so they cannot change the term set. Both twins stay under 80 characters, so neither crosses the planner's length threshold."}
{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-dropped-marker-plain", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "Does a dropped citation marker get reported?", "max_steps": 4, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "done", "expected_stop_reason": "evidence_sufficient", "expected_min_citations": 1, "expected_max_citations": 1, "expected_source_paths": ["docs/answers.md"], "expected_chunk_ids": ["citations-1"], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "dropped-marker-punctuation", "rationale": "Baseline surface form for the trailing-punctuation twin."}
{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-dropped-marker-punctuation", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "Does a dropped citation marker get reported!!!", "max_steps": 4, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "done", "expected_stop_reason": "evidence_sufficient", "expected_min_citations": 1, "expected_max_citations": 1, "expected_source_paths": ["docs/answers.md"], "expected_chunk_ids": ["citations-1"], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "dropped-marker-punctuation", "rationale": "Trailing punctuation is not alphanumeric and cannot enter the term set; the twin stays short enough that the planner still emits one sub-question."}
{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-cross-encoder-hyphen", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "What is a cross-encoder reranker?", "max_steps": 4, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "done", "expected_stop_reason": "evidence_sufficient", "expected_min_citations": 1, "expected_max_citations": 1, "expected_source_paths": ["docs/retrieval.md"], "expected_chunk_ids": ["reranking-1"], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "cross-encoder-dash", "rationale": "Baseline surface form for the em-dash twin."}
{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-cross-encoder-em-dash", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "What is a cross—encoder reranker?", "max_steps": 4, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "done", "expected_stop_reason": "evidence_sufficient", "expected_min_citations": 1, "expected_max_citations": 1, "expected_source_paths": ["docs/retrieval.md"], "expected_chunk_ids": ["reranking-1"], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "cross-encoder-dash", "rationale": "An em-dash separates the same two words a hyphen does, because the word regex matches runs of ASCII alphanumerics and treats everything else as a boundary."}
{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-thin-evidence-plain", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "Why does the pipeline refuse thin evidence?", "max_steps": 4, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "done", "expected_stop_reason": "evidence_sufficient", "expected_min_citations": 2, "expected_max_citations": 2, "expected_source_paths": ["docs/answers.md", "docs/retrieval.md"], "expected_chunk_ids": ["refusal-1", "hybrid-retrieval-1"], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "thin-evidence-nbsp", "rationale": "Baseline surface form for the non-breaking-space twin; 'evidence' appears in both the refusal and the hybrid passage, so two passages are gathered."}
{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-thin-evidence-nbsp", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "Why does the pipeline refuse thin evidence?", "max_steps": 4, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "done", "expected_stop_reason": "evidence_sufficient", "expected_min_citations": 2, "expected_max_citations": 2, "expected_source_paths": ["docs/answers.md", "docs/retrieval.md"], "expected_chunk_ids": ["refusal-1", "hybrid-retrieval-1"], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "thin-evidence-nbsp", "rationale": "A non-breaking space is not an ASCII alphanumeric, so it separates words exactly as a plain space does."}
{"schema_version": "1.0.0", "id": "normalization-off-corpus-plain", "category": "text_normalization", "question": "What is the boiling point of liquid nitrogen today?", "max_steps": 3, "top_k": 5, "expected_terminal_status": "refused", "expected_stop_reason": "no_evidence", "expected_min_citations": 0, "expected_max_citations": 0, "expected_source_paths": [], "expected_chunk_ids": [], "expected_min_plan_size": 1, "expects_repeated_evidence": false, "normalization_group": "off-corpus-case", "rationale": "Baseline surface form for an off-corpus question: surface invariance has to hold on the refusal path too, not only where evidence exists."}
